policy is about the name, which that licence does not grant.

What the name means
  "hibr" names the shell, the desktop and the modules released by Omar Sakka
  from the official sources: the git repository at
  https://github.com/osakka/hibr and the signed package repositories it
  publishes (apt, dnf, pacman and the application catalogue). A copy built
  from those sources, unchanged, is hibr.

What you may do without asking
  - Use the name to refer to hibr truthfully: write about it, review it,
    teach it, say that your software runs on it or works with it.
  - Redistribute official, unmodified releases under the name.
  - Package an official release for an operating system, with only the
    changes needed to build and install it there, under the name, saying
    whose package it is.

What needs permission
  - Calling a modified version, or a fork, "hibr", or anything likely to be
    confused with it ("hibr-plus", "HibrOS" and the like).
  - Using the name for a product, service, company or domain, or in
    a way that suggests Omar Sakka made or endorses something he did not.

Forks are welcome under the licence: give yours a name of its own, and say
that it is derived from hibr if you like. That much is all this asks.

